// The quadremesh package: automatic quad retopology. A closed or open mesh of
// triangles, quads or larger faces becomes a mesh of quads (quad-dominant: a
// few triangles are left where the flow could not close) at a face count or an
// edge length asked for, its rows following a smoothed orientation field. It is
// Instant Meshes (Jakob, Tarini, Panozzo and Sorkine-Hornung, "Instant
// Field-Aligned Meshes", 2015), Blender's Quad Remesh (QuadriFlow) and ZBrush's
// ZRemesher in what they do for an artist: the high mesh a sculpt, a boolean or
// a voxel remesh made, to the clean mesh a rig, a subdivision or a game wants.
//
//   import quadremesh use *;
//   mut o = RemeshOptions.withFaces(2000u32);
//   o.creaseAngle = 0.6;                       // keep edges sharper than this
//   imm r = quadRemesh(&source, &o);           // r.mesh is the PolyMesh
//   imm st = measure(&r.mesh, &source);        // quads, valences, Hausdorff ...
//
// Options (RemeshOptions)
// - 'faces' (aim) or 'edgeLength'; a lattice spacing from the area, so the
//   count comes out within a few per cent for a smooth shape.
// - 'creaseAngle' and 'creaseChannel' (an edge "crease" channel above 0), and
//   always the boundary of an open mesh: the result's rows run along them, its
//   vertices lie on them and a corner is a vertex. The edges found are written to
//   the result's "crease" edge channel, so subdivision keeps them sharp.
// - 'guides': pairs of source vertices joined by an edge (RemeshOptions.addGuide,
//   addGuidePath): the field must follow them, as ZRemesher's guide curves, and a
//   loop of them (an eye, a mouth) becomes a ring of quads.
// - 'density': a vertex channel of relative density (finer where larger, held
//   to a sixteenfold range: a fourfold change in edge length), as ZRemesher's
//   density painting.
// - 'symmetryAxis' and 'symmetryOffset': the plane the result is mirrored in.
//   One half of the source is remeshed, its cut held on the plane, and mirrored
//   and joined, so the two halves are exact mirror images. (The selection lane's
//   symmetry is not yet in the repository; this takes a plane.)
// - 'orientIterations', 'positionIterations', 'relax' (smoothing of the result
//   along the surface), 'tolerance' and 'maxPolygon' tune the method.
// - The result is projected back onto the source (sculpt's SurfaceIndex), and the
//   source's channels carried by sculpt's dataTransfer.
//
// The method (after Jakob et al.; the code is written from the paper)
// 1. The source is cut into triangles, edges longer than 0.3 of the lattice
//    spacing split (so every lattice cell holds several vertices), creases,
//    guides and the boundary marked as feature edges. Cotangent weights, held to
//    a sane range, are the graph's.
// 2. The vertices are merged in pairs by best score (normals alike, areas
//    small) level on level to about two dozen, constraints carried up.
// 3. The orientation field: at each vertex a unit tangent, equal to its quarter
//    turns (a 4-RoSy), smoothed by Gauss-Seidel from the coarsest level down,
//    each vertex taking the weighted sum of its neighbours' vectors turned to
//    the nearest quarter turn. A feature vertex's is fixed along the feature.
// 4. The position field: a point at each vertex; the lattice of the spacing
//    through it along the orientation is its reading of where vertices go.
//    Gauss-Seidel on the least distance between a point of one vertex's lattice
//    and a point of its neighbour's, from the coarsest level down. A feature
//    vertex's lattice is held to pass through it, a corner's to have a point
//    at it. (A first version took the lattices' points nearest the edge's
//    middle; that has the vertices following their own points as a fixed point
//    and never aligns: the distance between the lattices is what is minimised.)
// 5. Extraction: each source vertex rounds its position to its own lattice's
//    nearest point; neighbours rounding to the same point (within a tolerance
//    in lattice steps) are one output vertex, neighbours one step apart are
//    joined by an output edge. Edges lying along one another at a vertex are cut
//    to the better supported. The graph's faces are traced from its rotation
//    system (the edges at a vertex in order round its normal), and three to eight
//    sided faces kept.
// 6. Mending: larger faces are cut into quads round a middle vertex, holes of up to
//    ten edges that lie on the source are filled the same way, triangles next
//    to each other merged into a quad where it comes out between 35 and 145
//    degrees. Vertices are relaxed along the surface (feature vertices along
//    their feature) and put back onto the source.
//
// What it does not do
// - The flow is the field's, not the artist's: it has the singularities (valence
//   3 and 5) the field needs, on a sphere more than the eight a cube has, and
//   some isolated triangles.
// - A feature thinner than the lattice spacing is not resolved: the sealed lids
//   and lips of the MakeHuman head, the horns of the creature at 2k faces.
// - Creases are held exactly (the result lies on them), but the lattice cannot
//   be fitted to both ends of an edge, so a crease whose length is not a
//   multiple of the spacing has its last cells distorted, and at a coarse
//   spacing a face can span a crease.
// - No guarantee of manifold output: a face that would break a vertex's fan is
//   dropped and reported in 'problems'.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   work.cone       the working triangle mesh: triangulating, splitting long
//                   edges, clipping to a half, the boundary, normals and areas,
//                   the weighted graph
//   field.cone      the hierarchy, the orientation field, the position field
//   extract.cone    the clusters, the graph, its faces from the rotation system
//   assemble.cone   the mesh: polygons cut, holes filled, triangles paired,
//                   relaxing and projecting, mirroring, the PolyMesh
//   remesh.cone     RemeshOptions, Remesh, quadRemesh
//   measure.cone    QuadStats, measure

mod quadremesh;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import mesh use *;
import sculpt use *;
import time use Instant;
